#!/bin/sh
# go2rtc-net-hook — ifupdown if-up.d hook (issue #120, task #42).
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS
# The panel's FACTORY firewall lives in /etc/network/if-pre-up.d/iptables and, on every
# interface bring-up, does `iptables -F INPUT` then ~40 LOCK-LESS `iptables -A INPUT …`
# calls (no `-w`). go2rtcd's PERIODIC watchdog USED TO re-assert the GO2RTC :8554 rule each
# pass; when its writes overlapped the factory's lock-less rebuild, the factory's own `-A INPUT` calls FAILED
# SILENTLY and factory rules went missing on real hardware — the FTP-over-USB reflash port
# (used by MyHOME Suite), the factory security DROPs, and even the SSH ACCEPT after a reboot
# (locking SSH out until a power-cycle). The #42 fix has two halves:
#   A) go2rtcd's PERIODIC watchdog (`respawn`) no longer touches iptables AT ALL — every
#      iptables invocation (even a read-only `-S`) takes the xtables lock at launch, and the
#      watchdog fires on an independent timer that can't be sequenced against the factory's
#      rebuild. The firewall is (re)asserted ONLY on discrete events: start, stop, and B.
#   B) THIS hook re-asserts our rule at exactly the right moment for the events that bite.
#
# ifupdown runs if-pre-up.d scripts BEFORE the interface is up and if-up.d scripts AFTER, in
# the same event sequence. Living in if-up.d, this hook runs right AFTER the factory firewall
# rebuild — SEQUENTIALLY, with no competing daemon — so on boot and on WiFi-reconnect our
# single :8554 ACCEPT is restored without ever holding the xtables lock during the factory's
# lock-less writes. This hook is THE re-assert path for every factory rebuild (the factory
# only flushes INPUT on interface events, each of which runs this hook right after).
#
# It re-asserts the FIREWALL ONLY, via `go2rtcd fw-reassert`; it never starts or stops the
# daemon. It is best-effort and NON-FATAL: it must never fail an interface bring-up, so it
# always exits 0.
PATH=/sbin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/bin

# Re-assert after EVERY interface event, not just wlan0's. The factory if-pre-up.d/iptables rebuilds
# the WHOLE INPUT chain on ANY interface bring-up (see above), flushing our GO2RTC jump — so a usb0 (or
# any) bring-up would leave :8554 unreachable if we filtered to wlan0, and the periodic watchdog no
# longer re-asserts (task #42). fw-reassert always re-opens the wlan0 rule regardless of which
# interface's event fired it (firewall_open reads wlan0's own address) and is idempotent/zero-write when
# already correct, so running it on every event — including a harmless no-op on lo — is safe.

# Defensive: on a non-camera image go2rtcd is not installed (or not executable) — there is
# no GO2RTC rule to re-assert, so do nothing.
[ -x /etc/init.d/go2rtcd ] || exit 0

# Re-assert the GO2RTC firewall rule right after the factory rebuild. `fw-reassert` is quick
# and lock-bounded (flock mutex + `iptables -w 5`), so a plain best-effort call is safe; the
# `|| true` guarantees a non-zero exit (or a foreign/absent chain) can never fail the bring-up.
/etc/init.d/go2rtcd fw-reassert >/dev/null 2>&1 || true

exit 0
